11:35:41 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's actually a key logical mistake, and I want to point it out directly — not to prolong the argument, but because it's central to the decision you're making. You said: if a general statement is true about a group, then it should hold for pretty much any specific member, so exceptions don't matter. That's not how generalisations work. Take a statement almost everyone would accept as true: "Men are taller than women." That's true as a generalisation — on average, across populations. But it is completely useless for predicting the height of any *specific* man or woman you meet. A given woman can easily be taller than a given man, and that happens constantly. The general truth would be disastrous for, say, a shop that refused to sell tall-sized clothing to any woman because "women are generally shorter." Similarly, even if it were true that low-income families *on average* show less active educational prioritisation — and I want to be clear, I still don't accept that as the explanation — that would still tell you nothing about any specific family you're hoping to reach. A bursary doesn't go to an average. It goes to individuals. In the very catchment you want to skip, there will be families who are fighting tooth and nail for education against overwhelming odds. Skipping the entire postcode means skipping them too. That's the real-world consequence. Not a college seminar point. You'd be leaving behind the exact students the bursary is meant for, based on a group-level assumption that can't reliably identify those individuals. If you want to find the engaged families in that poorest catchment without wasting money on people who won't respond, the answer isn't to skip the catchment — it's to use a screening method that filters for the behaviour you're looking for. Ask schools for nominations of specific students whose families are visibly invested despite circumstances. Work through trusted community contacts who can point you to the ones showing up. That way, you don't reward disengagement, but you also don't punish the ones who are doing exactly what you want to see. That's a middle ground that doesn't require you to accept my overall view. It just requires acknowledging that generalisations mislead at the individual level. Does that feel workable?
